# parity.tcyr — replays the golden corpus (tests/golden/*.tsv) against the port.
#
# Port plan §4 step 7, adapted from ushma's tests/parity.tcyr (kimiya's, itself tanmatra's). Format and comparison
# rules: scripts/oracle/README.md. The module list is tests/golden/modules.txt, which the generator writes. The replay
# machinery is tests/parity_core.cyr and tests/ulp.cyr; this file holds the dispatchers. The math rows run on
# src/mathx.cyr (the correctly rounded primitives and the Rust-semantics helpers).
#
# Every row of every module is one of:
# - ok: every output matched within its class (A bit-identical, NaN matching any NaN; B <= 2 ulp; C 0 ulp against
#   the corrected oracle once a bound is set; D relative <= 1e-12; a zero carries the oracle's sign in every class);
# - FAILED: an output differs; the first 10 per module are printed with the row's note and both bit patterns;
# - measured: class C, with no bound set yet for its function. The ulp distance from the corrected oracle
#   (tests/golden/math.cr.tsv where it has the row, else the golden row) is recorded but not gated. None since M0
#   part 2c-2: every class-C function has its bound (docs/development/port-parity.md §2d);
# - accepted: a divergence an ADR accepts (_par_accepted), or a pass against the ADR 0003 corrected oracle (a row
#   glibc misrounds, where the port gives the correctly rounded value); a port value equal to glibc's misrounded one
#   on such a row FAILS, and is also counted as glibc-matching;
# - pending: its module or function has no Cyrius code yet (no dispatcher entry).
#
# Per function it prints the row counts and the ulp histogram. Shadow lines (`fn ~ candidate`) compare the same rows
# against another Cyrius candidate and are never gated. The exit code is the number of modules with a failure (at
# most a handful, so the 8-bit exit status can't wrap).
#
# What is wired up at M1 part 1:
# - `const` rows of every module: src/constants.cyr, src/constants_private.cyr and the scalar consts of the
#   generated data files, by Rust name;
# - the table rows (protein, alignment, enzyme, membrane, signal): answered from the generated src/*_data.cyr columns. A
#   row of a lookup over a table (`protein::lookup`, `enzyme::lookup_enzyme`, `alignment::substitution_score`) applies
#   the Rust function's rule to the generated data here, so every cell is compared through the oracle's API; at M2
#   and M3 those rows move to the ported `ry_*` functions;
# - `math` rows: classes A and B on the builtins (abs, ceil, sqrt), src/mathx.cyr's Rust-semantics helpers and
#   _ry_clamp and _ry_f64_to_u32 (in src/mathx.cyr since M1); class C (powf, exp, ln, exp_m1) on src/mathx.cyr's
#   correctly rounded primitives, at 0 ulp against the corrected oracle (ADR 0003). The stdlib's counterparts stay as
#   ungated shadow lines;
# - `guard` rows (M1): src/guard.cyr's validators (the error code, by Rust variant name, through src/error.cyr) and
#   sanitizers, class A;
# - `integrate` rows (M1): src/integrate.cyr, class B on the series branch and class C on the exp_m1 branch, at 0 ulp
#   against the corrected oracle (tests/golden/integrate.cr.tsv);
# - `logging` rows (M1 part 2): the level each rasayan event target gets from RASAYAN_LOG and the default
#   (src/logging.cyr's ry_log_level_of), class A; a form the port refuses takes the default, and where Rust's level
#   differs the row is accepted under ADR 0002 row 8;
# - `json` rows (M1 part 3a): serde_json's text for every scalar kind (src/json.cyr's writers) and Rust's Display of
#   an f64 (src/fmtf.cyr's `_ry_fmt_f64_display`), class A, byte for byte; (part 3b) serde_json's decision on each
#   document for each scalar kind and an all-f64 struct (src/json.cyr's readers), the value correctly rounded; a row
#   that fails where bayan refuses or misreads for a filed reason is accepted under ADR 0004.

include "tests/ulp.cyr"
include "src/constants.cyr"
include "src/constants_private.cyr"
include "src/mathx_data.cyr"
include "src/mathx.cyr"
include "src/protein_data.cyr"
